{
  "_source": "NXT Supplier Mapping 3.xlsx, 'Source Mapping' tab - the GREEN rows only, which the workbook's own legend defines as Mapped. Supersedes NXT Supplier Mapping_30Jul26.xlsx.",
  "_effective_date": "2026-07-31",
  "_label": "NXT Supplier Mapping 3 (31Jul26, green rows)",
  "_supersedes": "NXT Supplier Mapping 30Jul26 (green rows)",
  "_binding_rule": "Debayon Mallik, 31-Jul: 'for mapping we must consider the Source Table Column name (the last column of the mapping file) as the source columns.' That column holds the PHYSICAL column name in the extract (federal_reportable, legal_name); 'Source Column Name' holds the NetSuite UI label (1099 Eligible, Legal Name). Where the technical name is present it is used; where it is blank - every SyteLine row, because that sheet has no technical column - 'Source Column Name' is already the physical name (vend_num, addr##1) and is used as before. Against the real extract this takes NetSuite binding from 50/55 to 55/55.",
  "_extraction": "93 mappings from 129 green rows: 34 target 'DFF' or 'standard' rather than a named Oracle field and are excluded, and 2 are excluded with a reason recorded below.",
  "_scope": "Client NextPower. Keyed by SOURCE SYSTEM so NetSuite and SyteLine do not overwrite each other.",
  "_excluded_with_reason": [
    {
      "row": 65,
      "target_field": "Tax Registration Number",
      "source_column": "cnpj",
      "source_erp": "netsuite",
      "reason": "the cnpj column in Tracker_Netsuite_Supplier is CORRUPTED - 1,416 of its 1,449 values are Excel scientific notation ('3.54767E+13'), which has rounded the CNPJ to six significant digits and is not recoverable. The same identifier is intact in tax_number ('35.476.658/0001-59'), which is also a green row for this field, so that one is seeded instead. Loading cnpj would ship irrecoverably wrong Brazilian tax IDs for ~1,400 suppliers."
    },
    {
      "row": 169,
      "target_field": "Supplier Number",
      "source_column": "internal_id",
      "source_erp": "netsuite",
      "reason": "green fill but Bring to Oracle = no; the workbook's own comment says no data is available in the source extract and business input is required. The colour is a legend, the column is an instruction, so the instruction wins. NOTE: internal_id is in fact 100% populated in Tracker_Netsuite_Supplier (e.g. '-3'), so the comment and the extract disagree - this needs NextPower to confirm before it is imported."
    }
  ],
  "_open_questions": [
    "Prefix <- prefix: the column holds four values and all four are FULL PERSONAL NAMES ('ALAN RAJU NAIDU', 'Niranjani S'), not prefixes. Seeded as the workbook instructs because it is an explicit client mapping and the blast radius is four rows out of 5,476, but the Oracle Prefix field will carry names unless NextPower corrects it.",
    "Supplier Name <- legal_name and Alternate Name <- name: this workbook SWAPS the two relative to the 30-Jul edition (which had Alternate Name <- Legal Name and Supplier Name <- Name). It matches what the analyst typed into the steer box on 31-Jul, so the two agree, but it is a change of meaning rather than a rename and is called out here.",
    "E-Mail <- email and alt_email are both green for NetSuite. alt_email is 0% populated in Tracker_Netsuite_Supplier and email is 95.7%, so email is the live one.",
    "Taxpayer ID is fed by three green NetSuite columns - tax_id (35.3% populated), pan (5.7%) and tax_id_canada (0.2%). All three are seeded; the latest-date and transform-strength ordering decides, and a COALESCE rule would be the honest fix if NextPower confirms the precedence."
  ],
